WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:06.810
The COO is a role of someone who
needs to be a jack of many trades and

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.140
a master of many trades.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:09.760
That's a complex role.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.695
It's someone who can drive operational
excellence, but also be able to understand

00:00:14.695 --> 00:00:19.640
trends, create systems, put the resources
together to drive innovation.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:22.800
The Emerging COO: Driving Innovation and

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.812
Operational Excellence is a program for
current or aspiring COOs.

00:00:27.812 --> 00:00:33.358
It's a program that provides COOs
with frameworks to help them become

00:00:33.358 --> 00:00:39.381
more effective and successful in this
complicated and multifaceted role.

00:00:39.381 --> 00:00:44.876
Here at Stanford ,we are exceptionally
strong at teaching innovation,

00:00:44.876 --> 00:00:49.320
and we're exceptionaaly strong
at teaching operations.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.819
So we now have a framework that really
feeds the mindset of the COO and

00:00:54.819 --> 00:01:01.982
can help COOs achieve those two objectives
that historically were viewed as separate.

00:01:01.982 --> 00:01:04.818
Something that is unique
is this three month,

00:01:04.818 --> 00:01:08.172
blended experience that is
broke into three modules.

00:01:08.172 --> 00:01:10.971
We utilize a combination
of online learning,

00:01:10.971 --> 00:01:15.794
face to face learning, and experiential
learning back to your organization.

00:01:15.794 --> 00:01:20.414
But we begin with one month of
online experience with strategy and

00:01:20.414 --> 00:01:22.200
basics of finance.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.320
So our emerging COOs develop the language,
the vocabulary,

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:33.340
and the framework to be effective
partners with their CEO and their CFO.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:37.090
Then we come here for a week,
building on those foundations, and

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:38.990
expanding on those foundations.

00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:42.900
Then we bring in this notion of
operational excellence, innovation,

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.325
and how they come together through
a combination of case discussions and

00:01:47.325 --> 00:01:49.020
experiential sessions.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.100
And then at the end,
everyone will have the opportunity to take

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:59.220
an innovation slash operational
excellence challenge, work with a team

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:04.060
in their organization, tackle that
challenge using our frameworks and

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:08.570
our learnings with some support that
we can provide through that period.

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:13.443
Depending on where your organization is,
your role may be more focused on

00:02:13.443 --> 00:02:17.468
the operational excellence piece or
the innovation piece.

00:02:17.468 --> 00:02:20.789
So it's not like there
is one size fits all.

00:02:20.789 --> 00:02:24.082
COOs, oftentimes they
are unrecognized heros

00:02:24.082 --> 00:02:27.629
in the senior leadership
suite of organizations.

00:02:27.629 --> 00:02:29.252
They are the builders,

00:02:29.252 --> 00:02:34.455
the ones that work quietly behind
the scenes to make the organization work.

00:02:34.455 --> 00:02:38.698
And this is a very optimistic
place with a can-do attitude.

00:02:38.698 --> 00:02:42.609
COOs will find it hard
not to experience that.

